  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "If $A={0,1,2}$ and $B={x,y},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times A?$\n\n- $(1,y,2)$\n- $((1,y),2)$\n- $((y,1),y)$",
  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${0,1,2}$ with ${x,y},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/077da578323a686ecf0e07cf0a56030fa4ce128a74d408af95b8a3027ad13f28.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(0,x),(0,y),(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=3\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times A\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $A,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072443/assets/3a14abffc755dcfccce2d410ea18fbcae42d4afe2fbeffd81e1a291bc9d4968e.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times A|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |A|=6\\cdot 3=18.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((1,y),2)\\in (A\\times B)\\times A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
